(_pp.chapters = _pp.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"Paul and Timothy greet the saints, bishops, and deacons at Philippi. Paul thanks God for every remembrance of them, praying with joy for their fellowship in the gospel and confident that He who began a good work in them will finish it until the day of Jesus Christ; his prayer is that their love abound in knowledge and judgment. His circumstances have fallen out to the furtherance of the gospel: his bonds are manifest in the palace, and many brethren, emboldened by his chains, speak the word without fear. Some preach Christ of envy and strife, some of goodwill; either way, Christ is preached, and Paul rejoices. His outlook runs: for to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain. Torn between departing to be with Christ, far better, and abiding for their joy of faith, he expects to continue with them. The charge: stand fast in one spirit, with one mind, not terrified by adversaries, for to them it is given not only to believe but also to suffer for His sake.",
    context:"Paul, writing while imprisoned (probably at Rome), thanks the saints at Philippi, a congregation that supported him from the beginning of his ministry.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Philippians 1:6", text:"Being confident of this very thing, that he which hath begun a good work in you will perform it until the day of Jesus Christ:" },
      { ref:"Philippians 1:21", text:"For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain." },
      { ref:"Philippians 1:27", text:"Only let your conversation be as it becometh the gospel of Christ: that whether I come and see you, or else be absent, I may hear of your affairs, that ye stand fast in one spirit, with one mind striving together for the faith of the gospel;" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Christ can be magnified through life or death, so even chains advance the gospel.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where does Paul say his bonds have become manifest?", choices:["Only in his guard room","In the synagogues of Galatia","In the arena","In all the palace, and in all other places"], a:3, why:"Verse 13 says my bonds in Christ are manifest in all the palace, and in all other places." },
      { q:"What does Paul mean by to live is Christ, and to die is gain?", choices:["Death should be sought for its own sake","Life and death are meaningless","Living means serving Christ, and dying brings him to be with Christ, which is far better","Only martyrdom counts as faith"], a:2, why:"Verses 21-23 explain the strait betwixt two: a desire to depart and to be with Christ, which is far better, versus abiding in the flesh, which is more needful for them." },
      { q:"According to Philippians 1, what is granted to believers on Christ's behalf?", choices:["Not only to believe on him, but also to suffer for his sake","Wealth and honor","Freedom from all conflict","A guaranteed long life"], a:0, why:"Verse 29 says unto you it is given in the behalf of Christ, not only to believe on him, but also to suffer for his sake." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"Paul pleads for unity: fulfil my joy by being likeminded, of one accord, doing nothing through strife or vainglory, but in lowliness of mind esteeming others better than yourselves. The ground of the appeal is the supreme example: let this mind be in you which was also in Christ Jesus, who, being in the form of God, made Himself of no reputation, took the form of a servant, humbled Himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross. Therefore God highly exalted Him and gave Him a name above every name, that at the name of Jesus every knee should bow and every tongue confess that Jesus Christ is Lord. From that pattern follow the charges: work out your own salvation with fear and trembling, for God works in you to will and to do; do all things without murmurings, blameless amid a crooked nation, shining as lights in the world. Paul hopes to send Timothy and now sends Epaphroditus, his brother and fellowsoldier, who nearly died for the work of Christ.",
    context:"Paul presents Christ's humility and exaltation as the pattern for unity and urges them to shine as lights.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Philippians 2:5", text:"Let this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus:" },
      { ref:"Philippians 2:8", text:"And being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross." },
      { ref:"Philippians 2:9", text:"Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Let Christ's mind — humble, serving, obedient — be in you, for God exalts the lowly.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Whom does Paul trust in the Lord to send shortly, having no man likeminded who will naturally care for their state?", choices:["Epaphroditus","Timotheus","Tychicus","Luke"], a:1, why:"Verses 19-20 say I trust in the Lord Jesus to send Timotheus shortly unto you, for I have no man likeminded, who will naturally care for your state." },
      { q:"What did Christ do, being in the form of God?", choices:["Made himself of no reputation, took the form of a servant, and became obedient unto death","Refused to become human","Fled the cross by an angel's help","Ruled from heaven without suffering"], a:0, why:"Verses 6-8 trace Christ's humility: he made himself of no reputation, took the form of a servant, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross." },
      { q:"What does Paul urge as the pattern for our attitude toward others?", choices:["Insist on your rights","Measure others' faults","In lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves","Compete for positions"], a:2, why:"Verses 3-4 teach lowliness of mind, esteeming others better than ourselves and looking not only on our own things." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"Rejoice in the Lord, Paul repeats — writing the same things is safe. Beware of dogs, evil workers, and the concision: the true circumcision worship God in the spirit and have no confidence in the flesh. If credentials counted, Paul outranked them all: circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of the Hebrews, a Pharisee, zealous enough to persecute the church, blameless in the law's righteousness. But whatever things were gain to him, he counted loss for Christ, to be found in Him, not with a righteousness of his own from the law, but that which is of God by faith, that he might know Christ, the power of His resurrection, and the fellowship of His sufferings. Not that he had already attained or was already perfect: forgetting the things behind, he presses toward the mark for the prize of the high calling of God. Our conversation is in heaven, from which we await the Saviour, who shall change our vile body to be fashioned like His glorious body.",
    context:"Paul renounces his own credentials for the righteousness of faith and presses toward the prize of the high calling.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Philippians 3:8", text:"Yea doubtless, and I count all things but loss for the excellency of the knowledge of Christ Jesus my Lord: for whom I have suffered the loss of all things, and do count them but dung, that I may win Christ," },
      { ref:"Philippians 3:14", text:"I press toward the mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus." },
      { ref:"Philippians 3:20", text:"For our conversation is in heaven; from whence also we look for the Saviour, the Lord Jesus Christ:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Count every credential as loss for Christ and press toward the prize of His high calling.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Of which tribe does Paul say he is descended?", choices:["Judah","Levi","Benjamin","Ephraim"], a:2, why:"Verse 5 lists his pedigree: circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, an Hebrew of the Hebrews, a Pharisee." },
      { q:"What happened to Paul's credentials in his accounting?", choices:["They were improved","They were hidden from the church","They became his authority to teach","Whatever things were gain to me, those I counted loss for Christ"], a:3, why:"Verse 7 states the exchange, and verse 9 adds that Paul desired to be found in Christ, not having his own righteousness which is of the law." },
      { q:"What does Paul do with the past as he runs the Christian race?", choices:["He rehearses it for credibility","Forgetting those things which are behind, he reaches forth and presses toward the mark","He waits until the past is resolved","He asks others to judge it"], a:1, why:"Verses 13-14 describe forgetting the things behind, reaching forth unto the things before, and pressing toward the prize of the high calling of God." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"The final chapter is a stream of practical joy. Paul urges Euodias and Syntyche to be of the same mind in the Lord. Then the well-known charges: rejoice in the Lord alway, and again I say, Rejoice; let your moderation be known to all, for the Lord is at hand; be careful for nothing, but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God — and the peace of God, which passes all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds. Think on whatsoever is true, honest, just, pure, lovely, and of good report, and do what you have learned and received, and the God of peace shall be with you. Paul has learned contentment in every state — I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me — and he thanks the Philippians, the only church sharing with him from the beginning, for their gift, an acceptable sacrifice, with the promise that his God shall supply all their need.",
    context:"Paul gives final exhortations to joy, prayer, and pure thinking, then thanks the Philippians for their gift.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Philippians 4:6", text:"Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God." },
      { ref:"Philippians 4:7", text:"And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus." },
      { ref:"Philippians 4:13", text:"I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Rejoice, pray with thanksgiving, think on the true and lovely, and God's peace will guard your heart.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which two women does Paul urge to be of the same mind in the Lord?", choices:["Euodias and Syntyche","Priscilla and Aquila","Lydia and Dorcas","Mary and Martha"], a:0, why:"Verse 2 says I beseech Euodias, and beseech Syntyche, that they be of the same mind in the Lord." },
      { q:"How does Paul describe the way to be free from anxiety?", choices:["By ignoring trouble","In every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving letting requests be made known unto God","By collecting more wealth","By fasting weekly"], a:1, why:"Verse 6 says be careful for nothing, but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving; then the peace of God keeps hearts and minds." },
      { q:"What has Paul learned about his circumstances?", choices:["Circumstances define joy","Abundance is dangerous","Poverty is holier than abundance","In whatsoever state I am, therewith to be content"], a:3, why:"Verse 11 says he has learned contentment in every state, and verse 13 adds I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me." }
    ] });
