(_co.chapters = _co.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"Paul, with Timothy, greets the saints at Colosse, thanking God for their faith in Christ, their love, and the hope laid up in heaven, which they learned from Epaphras, their faithful minister. His prayer is that they be filled with the knowledge of God's will, walking worthy of the Lord, and giving thanks to the Father, who delivered us from the power of darkness into the kingdom of His dear Son, in whom we have redemption through His blood. Then the towering description of Christ: He is the image of the invisible God; by Him all things were created; He is before all things, and by Him all things consist; He is the head of the body, the church, the firstborn from the dead, having the preeminence and making peace through the blood of His cross to reconcile all things to Himself. Even alienated enemies He has reconciled by His death — if they continue grounded and settled in the gospel's hope. Paul ministers the mystery now manifest among the Gentiles: Christ in you, the hope of glory.",
    context:"Paul writes to the saints at Colosse, a congregation founded by Epaphras rather than Paul, to present the supremacy of Christ against teachers who diminished Him.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Colossians 1:15", text:"Who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature:" },
      { ref:"Colossians 1:17", text:"And he is before all things, and by him all things consist." },
      { ref:"Colossians 1:27", text:"To whom God would make known what is the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles; which is Christ in you, the hope of glory:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Christ is the image of the invisible God, creator and head of all, complete for our reconciliation.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Through whom did the Colossians first learn the gospel?", choices:["Peter","Paul","Timothy","Epaphras, their faithful minister"], a:3, why:"Verse 7 names Epaphras our dear fellowservant, who is for you a faithful minister of Christ, as their teacher." },
      { q:"How does Colossians 1 establish Christ's supremacy over creation?", choices:["By him were all things created, he is before all things, and by him all things consist","He was the first angel","He conquered the Romans","He wrote the Old Testament"], a:0, why:"Verses 15-17 present Christ as the image of the invisible God, in whom all things were created, who is before all things and by whom all things consist." },
      { q:"What does Paul call the great mystery now made manifest among the Gentiles?", choices:["The rebuilding of the temple","The fall of Rome","Christ in you, the hope of glory","The coming of a new prophet"], a:2, why:"Verse 27 names the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles: which is Christ in you, the hope of glory." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"Paul labours in conflict for them and for Laodicea, that their hearts, knit together in love, may reach full assurance of understanding to acknowledge the mystery of God and of Christ, in whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge. The walk must match the reception: as you have received Christ Jesus the Lord, so walk in Him, abounding with thanksgiving. Then the warning: beware lest anyone spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, and not after Christ — for in Him dwells all the fulness of the Godhead bodily, and you are complete in Him, the head of all principality and power. In Him they were circumcised without hands, buried and risen with Him in baptism, as God blotted out the handwriting of ordinances against us and nailed it to His cross, triumphing over principalities and powers. Let no one judge them in meat, drink, or sabbath days, shadows of things to come, nor beguile them with voluntary humility and worshipping of angels or touch not, taste not rules, mere commandments of men.",
    context:"Paul warns against philosophy and human regulations because believers are complete in Christ, who triumphed over all powers.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Colossians 2:6", text:"As ye have therefore received Christ Jesus the Lord, so walk ye in him:" },
      { ref:"Colossians 2:9", text:"For in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily." },
      { ref:"Colossians 2:14", text:"Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us, which was contrary to us, and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross;" }
    ],
    takeaway:"You are complete in Christ, so do not let anyone capture you with human philosophy or man-made rules.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What image does Paul use for the record of ordinances that stood against us?", choices:["A stone tablet hidden in the temple","Blotted out and nailed to his cross","A chain around the neck","A book burned in fire"], a:1, why:"Verse 14 says God blotted out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross." },
      { q:"Why does Paul say the Colossians should not be judged in meat, drink, holydays, or sabbaths?", choices:["Because diet does not matter at all","Because those laws never came from God","Because such things are a shadow of things to come, but the body is of Christ","Because Rome forbade them"], a:2, why:"Verses 16-17 call the regulations a shadow of things to come, with the substance in Christ." },
      { q:"What is the antidote to being spoiled through philosophy and vain deceit?", choices:["Recognizing that in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily, and ye are complete in him","Learning more philosophies","Adding angel worship","Keeping more ordinances"], a:0, why:"Verses 8-10 warn against philosophy after the tradition of men, grounding completeness in Christ, in whom dwells all the fulness of the Godhead bodily." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"Since risen with Christ, seek the things above: set your affection on things above, for your life is hid with Christ in God, and when Christ, who is our life, shall appear, you shall appear with Him in glory. Therefore mortify the earthly members — fornication, uncleanness, covetousness, which is idolatry — and put off anger, wrath, malice, and filthy communication; lie not, having put off the old man and put on the new, where Greek and Jew, bond and free are nothing: Christ is all and in all. Instead, put on mercies, kindness, humbleness of mind, meekness, longsuffering; forbear and forgive one another as Christ forgave; above all, put on charity, the bond of perfectness, and let the peace of God rule in your hearts. Let the word of Christ dwell richly, and whatever you do in word or deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus. Household duties follow — wives and husbands, children and fathers, servants and masters — do it all heartily as to the Lord.",
    context:"Paul calls believers to seek things above, put off the old man, put on Christlike virtues, and order their households.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Colossians 3:2", text:"Set your affection on things above, not on things on the earth." },
      { ref:"Colossians 3:14", text:"And above all these things put on charity, which is the bond of perfectness." },
      { ref:"Colossians 3:23", text:"And whatsoever ye do, do it heartily, as to the Lord, and not unto men;" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Put off the old man, put on Christlike virtues, and do everything heartily as to the Lord.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does Paul call charity in Colossians 3?", choices:["The least of the gifts","A burden to carry","An optional virtue","The bond of perfectness"], a:3, why:"Verse 14 says above all these things put on charity, which is the bond of perfectness." },
      { q:"What does it mean to mortify your members which are upon the earth?", choices:["Put to death earthly sins like fornication, uncleanness, and covetousness, which is idolatry","Practice fasting weekly","Endure physical suffering","Reject the material world entirely"], a:0, why:"Verse 5 lists the earthly members to mortify: fornication, uncleanness, inordinate affection, evil concupiscence, and covetousness, which is idolatry." },
      { q:"What standard does Colossians 3 set for daily labor?", choices:["Work only when supervised","Whatsoever ye do, do it heartily, as to the Lord, and not unto men","Serve only to gain inheritance","Avoid all work under unbelieving masters"], a:1, why:"Verses 23-24 teach hearty work as to the Lord, knowing that of the Lord ye shall receive the reward of the inheritance: for ye serve the Lord Christ." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Paul closes with final charges. Masters must give their servants what is just and equal, knowing they also have a Master in heaven. Continue in prayer with thanksgiving, asking God to open a door of utterance to speak the mystery of Christ, for which Paul sits in bonds; walk in wisdom toward them that are without, redeeming the time, with speech always gracious, seasoned with salt, ready to answer every man. Messengers follow: Tychicus, a beloved brother and faithful minister, comes with Onesimus, a faithful and beloved brother, one of them; greetings come from Aristarchus, Marcus, and Justus, and from Epaphras, who labours fervently in prayer for their perfection in all the will of God, and from Luke the beloved physician and Demas. Salutes go to Laodicea, to Nymphas, and the church in his house; the letter is to be read there, and the epistle from Laodicea read at Colosse. The word for Archippus: take heed to the ministry thou hast received in the Lord, that thou fulfil it — and from Paul's own hand, remember his bonds.",
    context:"Paul closes with charges on prayer and gracious speech, greetings from his coworkers, and a word about Laodicea and Archippus.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Colossians 4:2", text:"Continue in prayer, and watch in the same with thanksgiving;" },
      { ref:"Colossians 4:6", text:"Let your speech be alway with grace, seasoned with salt, that ye may know how ye ought to answer every man." },
      { ref:"Colossians 4:17", text:"And say to Archippus, Take heed to the ministry which thou hast received in the Lord, that thou fulfil it." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Pray for open doors, speak with grace seasoned with salt, and finish the ministry God gives you.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How does Paul describe Luke in his greetings?", choices:["The beloved scribe","The faithful centurion","The beloved physician","The quiet treasurer"], a:2, why:"Verse 14 says Luke, the beloved physician, and Demas greet you." },
      { q:"What qualities does Paul want in the believers' speech toward outsiders?", choices:["Silence and caution","Always with grace, seasoned with salt, knowing how to answer every man","Debate and persuasion","Flattery and humor"], a:1, why:"Verses 5-6 urge walking in wisdom toward them that are without, redeeming the time, with speech alway with grace, seasoned with salt." },
      { q:"What message does Paul send to Archippus?", choices:["Come to Rome at once","Lead the church in Laodicea","Write a letter to Nymphas","Take heed to the ministry which thou hast received in the Lord, that thou fulfil it"], a:3, why:"Verse 17 says say to Archippus, take heed to the ministry which thou hast received in the Lord, that thou fulfil it." }
    ] });
